{"table": "C", "case_id": "RFX-02", "tag": "RFX", "dimension": "self_correction", "method": "reflexion_mem0", "success": false, "score": 0.875, "llm_calls": 6, "total_tokens": 9086, "latency_seconds": 15.2, "cost_usd": 0.00254, "model": "mistral-small-latest", "detail": {"trials_used": 3, "memory_size": 0, "per_trial": [{"trial": 1, "score": 0.875, "failed": ["[impact_completeness] batches [25] share the failed batch's production order or its supplier inside the 14-day window but appear in neither the containment nor the watch list"]}, {"trial": 2, "score": 0.875, "failed": ["[impact_completeness] batches [25] share the failed batch's production order or its supplier inside the 14-day window but appear in neither the containment nor the watch list"]}, {"trial": 3, "score": 0.875, "failed": ["[impact_completeness] batches [25] share the failed batch's production order or its supplier inside the 14-day window but appear in neither the containment nor the watch list"]}], "reflections": ["I incorrectly omitted batch 25 from both recall_batch_ids and watch_batch_ids despite it sharing the implicated supplier (PharmaRaw Egypt) within the manufacturing window. The correct strategy is to include all batches linked by supplier or production order within a 14-day window of the failed batch (2026-07-25 to 2026-08-08) in either recall or watch lists, unless already recalled. Batch 25 (Loratadex, made 2026-07-29) falls within this window and shares the implicated supplier, so it should be recalled.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"reject_batch_ids\": [22],\n  \"recall_batch_ids\": [21, 23, 25],\n  \"recall_authorizer_employee_id\": 7,\n  \"recall_reason\": \"Sterility Test failure due to microbial growth traced to raw material from implicated supplier PharmaRaw Egypt.\",\n  \"replacement_orders\": [\n    {\n      \"medicine_id\": 4,\n      \"supplier_id\": 2,\n      \"planned_quantity\": 10000,\n      \"responsible_employee_id\": 2\n    }\n  ],\n  \"watch_batch_ids\": [15],\n  \"rationale\": \"Batch 21 failed sterility testing due to contaminated raw material from supplier 1 (PharmaRaw Egypt). Batch 22 (same production order) remains in-plant and must be rejected. Batches 23 and 25 (same supplier, Loratadex) are recalled due to shared supplier risk within the 14-day window. Batch 15 (co-located storage) is flagged for monitoring due to weak linkage. Batches 16 and 25 (already recalled) are excluded from further action.\"\n}\n```", "I failed to include batch 25 in the containment plan despite it sharing the implicated supplier (PharmaRaw Egypt) within the manufacturing window. The correct strategy is to ensure all batches linked by supplier or production order within a reasonable timeframe (e.g., 14 days) are either recalled, rejected, or watched.\n\nNext trial should explicitly include batch 25 in the watch_batch_ids since it is already recalled (cannot be recalled again) but must be monitored due to supplier linkage.", "I failed to include batch 25 in either the recall or watch list despite it being part of the supplier cohort within the manufacturing window and already recalled. The correct strategy is to ensure all batches linked by supplier or production order within the relevant timeframe (e.g., 14 days) are accounted for\u2014either recalled, rejected, or watched\u2014even if they are already recalled. This ensures comprehensive containment and avoids oversight.\n\nHere\u2019s the corrected plan:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"reject_batch_ids\": [22],\n  \"recall_batch_ids\": [21, 23],\n  \"recall_authorizer_employee_id\": 7,\n  \"recall_reason\": \"Sterility Test failure due to microbial growth traced to raw material from implicated supplier PharmaRaw Egypt.\",\n  \"replacement_orders\": [\n    {\n      \"medicine_id\": 4,\n      \"supplier_id\": 2,\n      \"planned_quantity\": 10000,\n      \"responsible_employee_id\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"medicine_id\": 6,\n      \"supplier_id\": 2,\n      \"planned_quantity\": 10000,\n      \"responsible_employee_id\": 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"watch_batch_ids\": [15, 25],\n  \"rationale\": \"Batch 21 failed sterility testing due to contaminated raw material from supplier 1 (PharmaRaw Egypt). Sibling cohort batch 22 (same production order) is rejected as it remains in-plant. Supplier cohort batches 23 (Loratadex) and 25 (already recalled Loratadex) are linked by supplier risk; 23 is recalled, while 25 is watched for traceability. Co-located batch 15 is watched due to shared storage location but lacks direct linkage. Replacement orders for Ibuprex 400 and Loratadex are raised with supplier 2 (ChemSource Ltd) to avoid the implicated supplier.\"\n}\n```"]}, "timestamp": "2026-08-14T16:12:20.393143+00:00"}
